-- Q1. What is a trigger in PL/SQL?
-- Ans: Trigger ek stored PL/SQL block hai jo
-- specified database event par automatically execute hota hai.

-- Q2. What is the purpose of SALARY_HISTORY?
-- Ans: Is table mein employees ki salary changes
-- ka history record store hota hai.

-- Q3. What does AFTER UPDATE OF BASICSAL mean?
-- Ans: BASICSAL column ko update karne ke baad
-- trigger execute hota hai.

-- Q4. What is :OLD.BASICSAL?
-- Ans: Ye employee ki update se pehle wali salary batata hai.

-- Q5. What is :NEW.BASICSAL?
-- Ans: Ye employee ki update ke baad wali salary batata hai.

-- Q6. Why is FOR EACH ROW used?
-- Ans: Isse trigger har affected employee row ke liye execute hota hai.

-- Q7. What is SYSDATE?
-- Ans: SYSDATE database server ki current date aur time return karta hai.

-- Q8. Does a trigger need to be called manually?
-- Ans: Nahi, salary update hone par trigger automatically execute hota hai.

-- Q9. What is an audit or history table?
-- Ans: Ye table database changes ka record future reference ke liye store karti hai.

-- Q10. What is the purpose of COMMIT?
-- Ans: COMMIT transaction ke changes permanently save karta hai.

-- Q11. What happens if UPDATE affects no rows?
-- Ans: Koi employee update nahi hota, isliye row-level trigger execute nahi hota.

-- Q12. What does :OLD.EID represent?
-- Ans: Ye employee ki existing row ka ID represent karta hai.

-- Q13. Can a trigger store data in another table?
-- Ans: Haan, trigger ke andar INSERT statement use karke doosri table mein data store kar sakte hain.

-- Q14. What is the purpose of SHOW ERRORS?
-- Ans: Isse trigger compile karte waqt aayi errors check kar sakte hain.

